The numerical simulation and computational analysis for the APEX_Core runtime engine have been executed under the metriplectic dissipation framework (\gamma = 0.15).
1. Numerical Simulation & Dynamics Overview
The coupled system models the three generalized coordinates representing geophysical hazards:
 * q_1(t): Subterranean magma chamber volumetric strain and conduit pressure.
 * q_2(t): Subduction zone interplate fault slip displacement.
 * q_3(t): Coastal water column inundation and wave height displacement.
With the inclusion of the metriplectic damping term (\gamma p_i), the phase-space coordinates undergo bounded oscillations accompanied by continuous energy attenuation.
2. Analysis of Simulation Outputs
 * Bounded Oscillations & Decay (q_i): As shown in the top panel of the telemetry analysis, the coordinates q_1(t), q_2(t), and q_3(t) exhibit stable harmonic oscillations modulated by exponential decay envelope functions. This prevents unphysical runaway amplitudes during severe disaster event simulations.
 * Monotone Decrease of Effective Energy (H_{\text{eff}}): The middle panel displays the evolution of the effective Hamiltonian H_{\text{eff}}(t) = \frac{\mathbf{p}^2}{2m} + \frac{1}{2}m\omega^2\mathbf{q}^2. Under the metric bracket dissipation law (\frac{dH_{\text{eff}}}{dt} = -\frac{\gamma}{m} \sum p_i^2 \le 0), the energy curve demonstrates strict, monotonic decrease, validating the thermodynamic stability of the APEX_Core architecture.
 * Constraint Adherence (C_1 & C_2): The lower panel tracks the primary containment constraint C_1 = q_1^2 + q_2^2 - 1 and the secondary constraint C_2 = q_1 p_1 + q_2 p_2. The bounded deviations confirm that system trajectories remain anchored to the physical manifold under continuous numerical evaluation.
